INT. FENCING SALLE - FORMER TRAM DEPOT - NIGHT

A long iron hall where trams once slept. Rails still run through the floor, buffed by a century of boots. Under the arched glass roof, two strips of copper mesh glow in pools of light. A scoreboard ticks. Benches line the walls, empty except for the shadows of rafters.

TWO FENCERS in white face each other across the strip, masked, guards up. The taller, KONSTANTIN VESELY, 30s, slow-moving and heavy-shouldered, a champion past his prime, shifts his weight and the floor creaks. The other, a YOUNG FENCER, 20s, light as a cat, a rival with nothing to lose, bounces on the balls of his feet. Between them at the strip's edge, a FENCING MASTER named ODALYS PRIEUR, 50s, in a gray blazer and black armband, raises one hand.

ODALYS
En garde.

They crouch. Blades touch, a thin chime.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Prêts?

Nobody answers. She lowers her hand.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Allez.

The young fencer lunges. Konstantin parries, a flash of steel, and ripostes. The young man's mask jerks. A green light flares on the scoreboard. Odalys raises her hand again.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Touché. One to Vesely.

The young fencer steps back, shaking his head. He rolls his wrist. Konstantin does not celebrate. He tilts his blade, studying its tip, and when he looks up he sees the younger man's eyes through the mesh, hard and bright.

They go again. Steel on steel. The younger man attacks high, low, high, and Konstantin yields ground until his heel hits the rail in the floor and he stumbles. A hit to the chest. A red light. The young fencer tears off his mask, sweat flying, face flushed.

YOUNG FENCER
That was mine.

ODALYS
It was, Mr. Banda.

KONSTANTIN
(from behind the mask)
You dragged your foot.

JOSEPHAT
I did not.

KONSTANTIN
You did.

ODALYS
Gentlemen.

JOSEPHAT
He's lying.

KONSTANTIN
I'm tired of lying. I'm saying you dragged your foot.

Odalys steps between them. She looks from one to the other. Konstantin removes his mask and the man beneath is sallow, damp, forty-two years old. He rubs his eyes with the heel of his glove.

KONSTANTIN (CONT'D)
My knee is shot. I'll admit that.

JOSEPHAT
Then retire.

KONSTANTIN
I will, when I lose.

JOSEPHAT
You're losing.

KONSTANTIN
Not yet.

ODALYS
Take your marks.

They don the masks. Take their places. Behind them the glass roof ticks with cooling air. The scoreboard reads four-all.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Final touch. En garde.

Konstantin sets his feet. Josephat drops into a crouch. For a moment the hall is so quiet that the clock can be heard turning inside the scoreboard.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Allez.

They explode. Steel sings. Josephat feints low, Konstantin ignores it, steps through, and his blade whips across Josephat's forearm in a curl so fast the eye can't follow. The green light flares. Josephat freezes, mouth open. Konstantin lowers his foil.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
Touché. Vesely wins.

Silence. Then Josephat tears his mask off and hurls it across the strip. It skids to a stop against a bench. He stands with his chest heaving, his hand at his arm.

JOSEPHAT
(to himself)
No. No.

KONSTANTIN
Shake my hand.

JOSEPHAT
Not tonight.

KONSTANTIN
Then tomorrow.

Konstantin pulls off his own mask. His face is gray with exhaustion. He holds out his glove. Josephat looks at it. Then, slowly, deliberately, he takes it, and squeezes hard. Konstantin winces. Neither lets go.

ODALYS
That is enough.

They release. Konstantin flexes his hand. Josephat turns and walks down the long iron hall, his boots ringing on the tram rails.
